            {
              "id": "GO-2026-6603",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "[GO-2026-6603] HTTP/2 server memory exhaustion due to Trailer headers in net/http"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "HTTP/2 server memory exhaustion due to Trailer headers in net/http"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "When \"Trailer\" headers are sent by a client, the HTTP server internally uses the header values to populate the Request.Trailer map passed to the server handler. Because Request.Trailer is a map, each entry incurs memory overhead. For HTTP/2 servers, a malicious client can exploit this by sending a \"Trailer\" header that declares a large number of fields, causing the server to allocate a disproportionate amount of memory while bypassing Server.MaxHeaderValueCount and Server.MaxHeaderBytes limits. This exploit is not applicable for HTTP/1 servers, which do not support multiplexing a large number of requests over one TCP connection, and whose Server.MaxHeaderBytes are calculated differently."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-2026-6603",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2026-78659"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GO-2026-6610",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "[GO-2026-6610] HTTP/2 transport accepts malformed framing-related headers in net/http"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "HTTP/2 transport accepts malformed framing-related headers in net/http"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Historically, we have been rather lax about malformed framing-related headers in our HTTP/2 implementation, as they cannot interfere with HTTP/2 framing. However, this makes it possible for our HTTP/2 implementation to forward responses containing such headers to an HTTP/1 client when acting as a reverse proxy. If the HTTP/1 client also does not behave strictly enough, this can result in response smuggling."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-2026-6610",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2026-78660"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GO-2026-6611",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "[GO-2026-6611] Excessive CPU consumption from repeated initial window changes in net/http"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Excessive CPU consumption from repeated initial window changes in net/http"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A malicious HTTP/2 peer can cause excessive CPU consumption in the client or server by opening a large number of streams and then sending many small SETTINGS frames containing SETTINGS_INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE values."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-2026-6611",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2026-78669"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GO-2026-6612",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "[GO-2026-6612] Double flow control refund on HTTP/2 server streams in net/http"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Double flow control refund on HTTP/2 server streams in net/http"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The HTTP/2 server can refund connection-level flow control twice for the same data: Once when a client resets a stream (refunding data for any sent-but-unread portion of the stream), and again when a request handler reads the buffered data. A malicious client can exploit this to bypass the configured connection-level flow control limit (MaxReceiveBufferPerConnection). Total buffered data is still limited by the concurrent stream limit and stream-level flow control."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-2026-6612",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2026-78663"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GO-2026-6617",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "[GO-2026-6617] HTTP/2 server crash due to HPACK encoder race in net/http"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "HTTP/2 server crash due to HPACK encoder race in net/http"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "HTTP/2 servers could end up crashing due to inadvertently modifying its HPACK encoder concurrently. This happens because the server modifies the HPACK encoder from two goroutines without synchronization: one uses the encoder to encode a HEADERS frame as part of a response sent to a client and the other modifies the encoder's table size when handling a SETTINGS frame containing SETTINGS_HEADER_TABLE_SIZE that a client sends. A malicious client can repeatedly send a request while changing the header table size to crash the server."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-2026-6617",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2026-97032"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GO-2026-6629",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "[GO-2026-6629] Panic parsing crafted input in x/text/secure/precis in golang.org/x/text"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Panic parsing crafted input in x/text/secure/precis in golang.org/x/text"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The Nickname profile can panic with an out-of-bounds slice error when transforming crafted input into a short destination buffer."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-2026-6629",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2026-56851"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GO-2026-6860",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "[GO-2026-6860] Avoid quadratic complexity when coalescing text nodes in html in golang.org/x/net"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid quadratic complexity when coalescing text nodes in html in golang.org/x/net"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "When coalescing adjacent text nodes in addText and fosterParent, appending to an existing TextNode's Data via string concatenation (n.Data += text) reallocated and copied the accumulated string on every token. Inputs containing many short text runs separated by ignored end tags (such as \"a\u003c/x\u003e\") caused O(n^2) allocations and byte copies.\n\nAccumulate coalesced text in per-node byte slices during parsing and materialize Data strings once at the end of parse."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-2026-6860",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2026-78661"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GO-2026-6866",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "[GO-2026-6866] Check all ancestor namespace transitions when rendering raw text in html in golang.org/x/net"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Check all ancestor namespace transitions when rendering raw text in html in golang.org/x/net"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "In childTextNodesAreLiteral, walking up the tree previously stopped at the nearest foreign ancestor once an HTML integration point was found. However, that integration point may itself be a descendant of an element that was foster-parented into a foreign namespace (such as \u003cmglyph\u003e or \u003cmalignmark\u003e fostered under \u003cmtext\u003e), causing a parser to resolve the subtree in a different foreign namespace upon re-parsing the rendered output.\n\nContinue walking the full ancestor chain and verify that every transition from a non-empty namespace occurs at an HTML integration point before emitting literal text."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-2026-6866",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2026-97045"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      },
